WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.440
How does the time reset actually&nbsp;
work? Why are they called "mimics"?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.089
Here are the answers to your biggest&nbsp;
questions about "Edge of Tomorrow".

00:00:08.089 --> 00:00:12.780
In the film's opening montage, we learn that&nbsp;
a meteor has crashed to Earth, bringing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:17.340
it an invasive extraterrestrial species that&nbsp;
humans call "Mimics." They've been spreading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.800
across Europe for years, easily overcoming all&nbsp;
conventional military defenses. To combat this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.000
alien menace, the world's governments join&nbsp;
together to form the United Defense Force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.260
or UDF, and begin manufacturing suits&nbsp;
of powered armor for their infantry.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.200
When the UDF has their first&nbsp;
victory at the Battle of Verdun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.740
the belief is that the new mech suits&nbsp;
have proven their worth. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.120
Sgt. Rita Vrataski was able to use one to&nbsp;
perform incredible and daring feats despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.620
limited experience, proving that anyone with&nbsp;
minimal training can operate one efficiently.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.440
"With the new jacket technology&nbsp;
and minimal training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.300
we've been able to create super soldiers."

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.680
In actuality, Rita's apparently instant&nbsp;
mastery of the armor and the battlefield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.980
were the result of her ability to&nbsp;
repeat any day in which she dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.180
giving her unlimited replays of any&nbsp;
deadly scenario. Since the UDF is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:07.560
unaware of this — and refused to believe it&nbsp;
in any of Rita's previous lives — they move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.760
forward confidently with an aggressive&nbsp;
invasion of France, Operation: Downfall.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.900
However, Rita and her ally Dr. Carter&nbsp;
suspect there's still another layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.920
to the Battle of Verdun. They believe&nbsp;
that the Mimics eventually allowed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.400
UDF to win at Verdun in order to lend&nbsp;
them a false sense of confidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.360
prompting an all-out assault that would&nbsp;
allow the Mimics to annihilate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.920
UDF in a single stroke. It's also possible that&nbsp;
the Mimics accepted the result of the Battle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.527
Verdun that ended with Rita losing her ability&nbsp;
to reset, deciding that this was victory enough.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.480
Throughout the story of "Edge of Tomorrow," the&nbsp;
shape-shifting alien menace that's conquering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.640
Earth is always referred to as the "Mimics." It's&nbsp;
a cool-sounding name that helps to uniquify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.380
film's villain, avoiding the constant reuse&nbsp;
of the words "alien" or "enemy." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.220
although the Mimics do look and move&nbsp;
in a strange, ever-changing way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.180
it's never exactly clear who or&nbsp;
what it is they're "mimicking."

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.960
To find an answer to this question, you'd&nbsp;
have to go back to the source material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.640
the light novel "All You Need is&nbsp;
Kill" by Hiroshi Sakurazaka. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.940
it's explained that the aliens&nbsp;
based their earthly shape on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.020
first life form they encountered after&nbsp;
crash-landing on our planet: a starfish.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:20.820
This idea isn't really represented in the film,&nbsp;
with the Mimics receiving a totally original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:26.460
design that doesn't map easily onto anything in&nbsp;
our animal kingdom. Their forms are constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:32.040
changing, their metallic tentacles forming into&nbsp;
legs or sharp spikes, or unraveling so that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:37.020
can rest flat in the dirt beneath their prey.&nbsp;
Since the Mimics' bodies are incredibly fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:43.200
and perpetually reshaping themselves, the audience&nbsp;
rarely gets a good, long look at them. Ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.360
this makes them one of the more interesting and&nbsp;
memorable alien designs in recent memory, since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.440
we get an idea as to what they look like, but&nbsp;
would struggle to describe them to another person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:58.121
"Starfish" doesn't really cut it, and neither does&nbsp;
any other comparison. "Mimic" will have to do.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:03.060
When Cage rescues Sgt. Rita Vrataski on&nbsp;
the battlefield, demonstrating a clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.535
foreknowledge of the dangers around them,&nbsp;
Rita realizes that he must be on a time loop.

00:03:07.535 --> 00:03:09.484
"Find me when you wake up."

00:03:09.484 --> 00:03:15.420
In his next life, Cage finds Rita and learns&nbsp;
that, months earlier, she also absorbed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.680
blood of an Alpha Mimic, gaining the ability&nbsp;
to start the day over upon her death. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.360
she lost that ability on her last loop, during&nbsp;
which she managed to win the Battle of Verdun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:29.520
but also suffered serious, non-lethal blood&nbsp;
loss. When she received a transfusion of new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.520
normal blood, she lost the&nbsp;
ability to reset the day.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:37.740
Upon hearing this, we can't help but ask one&nbsp;
logical question: How can Rita possibly know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:42.900
that she no longer resets time when she dies,&nbsp;
unless she died and didn't come back? This,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.620
unfortunately, is something the viewer simply&nbsp;
has to accept in order for the movie to work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:51.180
To patch up this potential plot hole, we're&nbsp;
forced to accept that Rita, and the later Cage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:55.920
can simply feel that their ability to reset&nbsp;
time is gone. We see it happen to Cage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.840
after he suffers his own similar injury and&nbsp;
blood transfusion in the final loop of the film.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.880
Is it beyond belief that Mimic Alphas would&nbsp;
have such a sixth sense that allows them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.840
know whether or not they're detached from the&nbsp;
time stream? Not entirely. One can imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.940
that such an instinct might have evolved within&nbsp;
Mimics in order to alert an Alpha that it's lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:20.379
its metaphysical save state. Still, it's certainly&nbsp;
convenient for our heroes — and for the audience.

00:04:20.379 --> 00:04:24.360
Dr. Carter does a pretty solid job&nbsp;
explaining the mechanics of "Edge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.560
Tomorrow's" time travel during his initial&nbsp;
meeting with Cage. While most Mimics are of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.700
the animalistic soldier variety, there are&nbsp;
two special kinds of Mimic that, together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:37.860
give them their unique advantage over humanity.&nbsp;
Their movements are governed by a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:43.740
Omega Mimic, a strange and stationary creature&nbsp;
with the power to skip backwards in time by about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:49.020
one day. However, the Omega can only do this when&nbsp;
its counterpart, the Alpha Mimic, is killed. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:54.480
is basically a defensive reflex. While the Omega&nbsp;
remains hidden, the Alpha goes into battle. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.520
Alpha is killed, that means a battle has not gone&nbsp;
as well as possible and should be attempted again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.620
with the benefit of this experience. In short:&nbsp;
the Mimics are save-scumming the war for Earth.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:09.420
When Cage is infused with the Alpha's blood&nbsp;
on the battlefield, he acquires the Alpha's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:13.800
ability to trigger the Omega's time reset.&nbsp;
The Omega is still the one altering time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:19.680
but it's reflex is kicked every time Cage&nbsp;
dies, instead of when the Alpha dies. Cage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.780
gets the added benefit of countless replays and&nbsp;
accumulated experience, giving him an advantage in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:30.749
battle. Like Rita before him, Cage only loses this&nbsp;
power once enough of his blood has been replaced.

00:05:30.749 --> 00:05:35.160
Because the Mimics' activities and motivations&nbsp;
are kept a mystery from the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.560
we never definitively learn what effect Cage's&nbsp;
blood infusion has on the Mimics' own abilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.660
Whether or not the Alpha's death would still&nbsp;
trigger a time reset while Cage has the power is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:51.360
never totally clear, since we don't see an Alpha&nbsp;
killed between the first and final versions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.400
Operation: Downfall. However, since the Mimics'&nbsp;
behavior remains predictable in the time loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.240
we can safely assume that they are not&nbsp;
retaining knowledge from each reset.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:06.180
The only changing factor is Cage's psychic visions&nbsp;
from the Omega, which grow stronger after each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:10.740
loop. This is how the Mimics eventually figure&nbsp;
out that Cage has become the new reset trigger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:15.480
setting a trap for him with a fake vision&nbsp;
of the Omega's location. In this loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:20.280
the Mimic Alpha corners Cage and deliberately&nbsp;
keeps him from killing himself, apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:25.920
trying to capture him alive so as not to lose&nbsp;
this progress. Cage manages to reset anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.300
and the Mimics never seem to pay special&nbsp;
attention to him again. This implies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:34.548
they only know of his unique nature&nbsp;
on loops in which he gives it away.

00:06:34.548 --> 00:06:38.400
Before their final charge towards&nbsp;
the Omega during their final loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.660
Rita and Cage share a brief moment&nbsp;
during which Rita commends Cage on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.200
bravery during their work together.&nbsp;
Just before charging into battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:50.280
Rita kisses Cage on the lips, saying that&nbsp;
she wishes she'd gotten to know him better.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:55.080
It's not totally unreasonable to think that&nbsp;
Rita might want a last kiss before dying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:00.180
especially when the available, willing partner&nbsp;
is a fellow soldier who looks like Tom Cruise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:05.160
However, the moment rings a little false when you&nbsp;
consider that, while Cage has known Rita for years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.960
through the repetition of the time loop, Rita has&nbsp;
only known Cage for about a day and a half. True,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:13.980
there have been loops during which Cage and&nbsp;
Rita have grown close over the course of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:18.180
long road trip, but even then, Rita remains&nbsp;
an emotionally guarded person who hesitates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:23.700
to let herself feel for Cage. Because each loop&nbsp;
implies the repetition of some but not all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:28.440
the routines that Cage and Rita follow on their&nbsp;
usual paths, we actually don't see very much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:33.660
Cage and this version of Rita. So this is one of&nbsp;
the reasons why the kiss raises some eyebrows.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:39.000
In an essay for The Wire, film critic Esther&nbsp;
Zuckerman described the last-second romantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.960
twist to be a betrayal of the character&nbsp;
for the sake of offering Cage a reward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.040
for his personal growth over the course&nbsp;
of the film. Indeed, the moment plays as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:52.320
if it's more about demonstrating that Cage&nbsp;
is no longer the loathsome, unlovable weasel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:56.760
that we meet at the start of the film than&nbsp;
it is about Rita learning to trust again.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:02.040
As previously mentioned, the film is based on&nbsp;
a novel by Hiroshi Sakurazaka entitled "All You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:06.180
Need Is Kill." According to an interview with&nbsp;
Variety, Warner Bros. President of Marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:10.380
Sue Kroll found that audiences were put off&nbsp;
by movies with the word "Kill" in the title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:13.740
leading to the project's rebranding.&nbsp;
Director Doug Liman, on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.880
later told Den of Geek that the film was&nbsp;
renamed to accommodate the film's tone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:22.920
which is significantly lighter than that of the&nbsp;
source material. He suggested that the film be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:28.140
renamed "Live Die Repeat" instead. Someone at&nbsp;
Warner Bros. wasn't fond of this title, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:33.360
bequeathed the project the more generic-sounding&nbsp;
title "Edge of Tomorrow," against Liman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:38.220
objections. "Live. Die. Repeat." was used as a&nbsp;
tagline for the movie instead. Liman feels that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:42.480
this contributed to the film underperforming&nbsp;
at the box office despite critical acclaim.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:47.280
Someone must have listened, because when the&nbsp;
movie was released on home video months later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:52.140
"Live. Die. Repeat." was suddenly the larger&nbsp;
print on the box, and digital marketplaces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:56.520
listed the full title as "Live Die Repeat:&nbsp;
Edge of Tomorrow." Depending on when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:00.900
purchased a physical copy of the movie, it&nbsp;
may feature the two titles in a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:05.820
order. Doug Liman said that the sequel's&nbsp;
working title is "Live Die Repeat Repeat,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:10.830
and that its release would canonize "Live&nbsp;
Die Repeat" as the title of the first film.

00:09:10.912 --> 00:09:12.315
"What took you so long?"

00:09:12.315 --> 00:09:18.000
Once Cage has lost the ability to reset time,&nbsp;
he and Rita lead a suicide mission to destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:22.440
the Omega, which is hidden under the Louvre&nbsp;
museum in Paris. Their mission is successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:27.960
as Cage is able to drop a belt of live grenades&nbsp;
into the Omega's mouth. Cage and the Alpha are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:32.460
caught in the blast as well, and it appears&nbsp;
that Cage lives long enough to absorb some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:36.960
of their blood. Then, he wakes up at an earlier&nbsp;
point in the timeline than he's accustomed to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:41.220
before his disgraceful demotion to&nbsp;
Private, and learns that the Mimics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:45.180
have lost their capacity to fight the&nbsp;
war. Instead of a bloody trench battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:50.040
Operation: Downfall will now be met with minimal&nbsp;
resistance from the extraterrestrial menace.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:55.800
This seems awfully convenient for Cage, who has&nbsp;
now saved not only the world but his own career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:00.660
and reputation. The film didn't give us a clear&nbsp;
explanation as to why this has happened, but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:06.120
can extrapolate one from the logic of the film. It&nbsp;
may be that Cage absorbing the Omega's blood has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:11.160
different effect than just absorbing the Alpha's,&nbsp;
giving him more control over the time stream than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:15.960
he had as merely the trigger for the reset.&nbsp;
He may have instinctively selected a moment in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:20.760
time to return to that would put him in the best&nbsp;
possible position. Co-screenwriter Christopher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:25.827
McQuarrie promised in a since-deleted Tweet that&nbsp;
the sequel will concretely answer this question.

00:10:25.827 --> 00:10:27.000
"I'll see you tomorrow."

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:31.980
Arguably, one of the biggest points of confusion&nbsp;
surrounding "Edge of Tomorrow" was why it wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:37.320
really considered a hit! Before the film was even&nbsp;
released, the press was already prepared to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:42.060
off "Edge of Tomorrow" as a flop. Whether this&nbsp;
is the fault of the generic-sounding title or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:46.920
the marketing campaign's failure to convey the&nbsp;
high concept is still up for debate. In any case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:52.260
"Edge of Tomorrow" opened at #3 at the box&nbsp;
office, losing the weekend to tear-jerking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:56.640
romance "The Fault in Our Stars" and the&nbsp;
live-action Disney fantasy "Maleficent."

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:02.580
The film grossed about $100 million at the US&nbsp;
box office and another $270 million overseas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:07.920
but its reported budget was $178 million.&nbsp;
It's also worth noting that projecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:12.360
"Edge of Tomorrow" to be a failure fit into&nbsp;
a popular narrative that Tom Cruise's star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:17.340
was fading in the United States. His previous&nbsp;
three films, "Oblivion," "Jack Reacher," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:20.940
"Rock of Ages," were all considered&nbsp;
to have underperformed domestically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:25.560
so it's possible that the atmosphere around&nbsp;
the film's release was already poisoned.

